<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tansen, Nepal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen">Tansen</a> is a hill town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, the headquarters of Palpa District, on a ridge of the Mahabharat range. From the 16th century it was the capital of the Sen kings of Palpa, once the strongest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaubise-rajya">Chaubise Rajya</a>, until its annexation by the Gorkha kingdom in 1806. Newar traders settled there, and its temples and Tansen Durbar palace reflect that history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tansen-nepal.jpg" alt="Tansen, Nepal" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mithunkunwar9 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_View_of_Tansen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen">Tansen</a> stands on the highway between Butwal and Pokhara, on the crest of the Mahabharat Range or Lesser Himalaya, overlooking the valley of the Kaligandaki River to the north. The highway passes west of the town centre, a maze of steep, narrow and winding alleys lined with Newari shophouses and temples. From the nearby hill of Srinagar Danda the Kanjiroba, Dhaulagiri, Annapurna, Mansiri, Ganesh and Langtang groups of the high Himalaya are in view. The climate is moderate, rarely above 30 °C or below freezing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Palpa district is home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magar-people">Magar people</a>, and the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen">Tansen</a> comes from the Magar language, meaning northern settlement. In the 16th century, under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukunda-sen">Mukunda Sen</a>, the rulers of Tansen came close to conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>. The town later became a Newari bazaar on the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> that ran by way of the Kaligandaki River and Mustang.</p><p>As a district administrative centre of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, Tansen was targeted several times by Maoists during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepalese-civil-war">Nepalese Civil War</a>, including a major assault on the historic Durbar compound in 2006. The town was also affected by the earthquake of 25 April 2015.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen">Tansen</a> is a municipality and the administrative centre of Palpa District. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newars</a> are known for traditional metalwork and for dhaka cloth, used in traditional jackets and topis, and the octagonal pavilion called Golghar stands in Sitalpati, the main market square. The town was added to the UNESCO World Heritage Tentative List on 30 January 2008.</p><p>The United Mission Hospital, a mile east of the centre, was founded in 1954 by the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and a coalition of 20 Christian organisations and is the oldest project of the United Mission to Nepal. Nearby are Ranighat, a Rana palace beside the Kaligandaki, and Ridi Bazaar, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage place where a large fair is held on Maghe Sankranti.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tansen, Nepal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He is said to have campaigned as far as the Kathmandu Valley, and his realm, centred on <b>Tansen</b>, was later divided among his sons into Palpa, the Kingdom of Makwanpur and other states.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-palpa">Kingdom of Palpa</a></li></ul></section>
